Chapter: DiskTestRunner
  cites Foreword chapter Fat16
  cites Codex chapter Opening (compile-frontend, parse-mode-flags)
  cites Codex chapter Diagnostic Bag (bag-errors)
  cites Foreword chapter Maybe

 Build.md Phase B. The on-disk test runner: mount the boot volume, list what is
 on it, choose an entry by name, read the bytes back, and COMPILE them in this
 same program.

 The compiler arrives as a library. `Chapter: Opening` declares `codex-opening`
 and no `opening`, so it is citable, and `Codex` is a manifest quire: a cite of
 any of its chapters pulls the whole ordered unit `build/compiler-order.txt`
 names, minus that unit's own entry chapter. That is what lets one program
 compile another without a second boot.

 Every line prints a verdict of its own rather than one summary, because a
 bare-metal boot has no exit code to read: the serial log IS the result, and a
 stage that fails must say which stage.

Section: Reporting

  yn : Boolean -> Text
  yn (b) = if b then "yes" else "no"

Section: The Compile

 `compile-frontend` is pure and Console-free, so a verdict costs no effects and
 needs no act block. Errors are counted rather than printed: a bare-metal serial
 log is the only channel and a failing chapter can produce hundreds.

 The bag is read ONCE per entry and both numbers come out of that one read: a
 second call would compile the chapter again to learn what it already knew.

  RunnerResult = record {
   rr-errors : Integer,
   rr-code : Integer,
   rr-bytes : Integer,
   rr-emit-errors : Integer,
   rr-emit-code : Integer,
   rr-message : Text,
   rr-line : Integer,
   rr-col : Integer
  }

 The BACKEND runs here too, not the frontend alone. A chapter whose frontend is
 clean and whose codegen is not looks identical to a clean one when only the
 frontend runs, and that is the class no arm on the guest could observe.
 `compile-to-cdx` answers the emitted bytes in memory, so the runner reports a
 size without writing anything.

  RunnerEmit = record {
   re-bytes : Integer,
   re-errors : Integer,
   re-code : Integer
  }

  runner-emit-none : RunnerEmit
  runner-emit-none = RunnerEmit { re-bytes = 0, re-errors = 0, re-code = 0 }

  runner-emit : CompileFrontend -> RunnerEmit
  runner-emit (fe) =
   let r = compile-to-cdx fe
   in let ees = bag-errors (r.bag)
   in RunnerEmit {
    re-bytes = list-length (r.header-bytes) + (r.content-len) + list-length (r.tail-bytes),
    re-errors = list-length ees,
    re-code = if list-length ees == 0 then 0 else (list-at ees 0).code
   }

 Every subject is compiled between a heap mark and its restore, which is the
 shape the design ruled: a bare-metal heap has no collector, so without the
 restore each subject's AST, IR and emitted buffer stay resident and the run
 length is bounded by memory rather than by the corpus. Only Integers cross the
 restore, and the record below is allocated after it.

  runner-run : Text -> RunnerResult
  runner-run (t) =
   let hp = __heap-save
   in let fe = compile-frontend-cdx t "Program" (parse-mode-flags "CDX" (text-length t))
   in let es = bag-errors (fe.bag)
   in let n = list-length es
   in let code = if n == 0 then 0 else (list-at es 0).code
   in let msg = if n == 0 then "" else (list-at es 0).message
   in let ln = if n == 0 then 0 else ((list-at es 0).span.start.line)
   in let cl = if n == 0 then 0 else ((list-at es 0).span.start.column)
   in let em = if n == 0 then runner-emit fe else runner-emit-none
   in let bytes = em.re-bytes
   in let ecount = em.re-errors
   in let ecode = em.re-code
   in let restored = __heap-restore hp
   in RunnerResult { rr-errors = n, rr-code = code, rr-bytes = bytes, rr-emit-errors = ecount, rr-emit-code = ecode, rr-message = msg, rr-line = ln, rr-col = cl }

 A read that failed and a chapter that would not compile are different answers
 and a summary must not merge them, so an unreadable entry is -1 rather than an
 error count of its own.

  runner-of : Maybe Text -> RunnerResult
  runner-of (src) =
   if is-none src then RunnerResult { rr-errors = 0 - 1, rr-code = 0, rr-bytes = 0, rr-emit-errors = 0, rr-emit-code = 0, rr-message = "", rr-line = 0, rr-col = 0 }
   else runner-run (from-maybe src "")

 The FIRST diagnostic code is what a `.failing` sidecar records, so printing it
 is what lets that half of the diff be made at all. The pairing itself stays on
 the host: the sidecars are not on the image, and putting them there is a
 namespace question rather than a runner one.

  runner-line : Text, RunnerResult -> Text
  runner-line (nm) (r) =
   if r.rr-errors < 0 then nm & " : READ FAILED"
   else if r.rr-errors > 0 then nm & " : errors=" & show (r.rr-errors) & " ok=no first=CDX" & show (r.rr-code) & " at " & show (r.rr-line) & ":" & show (r.rr-col) & " -- " & (r.rr-message)
   else if r.rr-emit-errors > 0 then nm & " : frontend ok, CODEGEN errors=" & show (r.rr-emit-errors) & " first=CDX" & show (r.rr-emit-code)
   else nm & " : errors=0 ok=yes cdx=" & show (r.rr-bytes)

 EFI and SEED are the image's own directories, written by the image writer
 rather than put there by a caller. Everything else on the root is a subject,
 and a subject is either a source or a directory of them.

  runner-skip : Text -> Boolean
  runner-skip (n) =
   if n == "EFI" then True
   else if n == "SEED" then True
   else if n == "." then True
   else n == ".."

  runner-say : Text, RunnerResult -> [Console] Integer
  runner-say (nm) (r) = act
    print-line-uni (runner-line nm r)
    r.rr-errors
   end

  runner-subject : Fat16Volume, Text -> [Console, Device.Block] Integer
  runner-subject (vol) (path) = act
    src <- fat16-read-source vol path
    runner-say path (runner-of src)
   end

 The image writer places the sources in SRC0, SRC1 and so on when the caller
 asks for it, because the FAT16 root holds 509 usable entries and cannot hold
 two names that fold to one 8.3 spelling. One level is what the writer produces
 and one level is what this walks.

  runner-files : Fat16Volume, Text, List Fat16DirEntry, Integer, Integer, Integer -> [Console, Device.Block] Integer
  runner-files (vol) (dir) (es) (i) (n) (bad) =
   if i >= n then bad
   else let e = list-at es i
   in if fat16-is-dir-entry e then runner-files vol dir es (i + 1) n bad
   else act
    err <- runner-subject vol (dir & "/" & (e.de-name))
    runner-files vol dir es (i + 1) n (if err == 0 then bad else bad + 1)
   end

  runner-walk : Fat16Volume, List Fat16DirEntry, Integer, Integer, Integer -> [Console, Device.Block] Integer
  runner-walk (vol) (es) (i) (n) (bad) =
   if i >= n then bad
   else let e = list-at es i
   in if runner-skip (e.de-name) then runner-walk vol es (i + 1) n bad
   else if fat16-is-dir-entry e then act
     sub <- fat16-list-dir vol (e.de-name)
     deeper <- runner-files vol (e.de-name) sub 0 (list-length sub) bad
     runner-walk vol es (i + 1) n deeper
    end
   else act
    err <- runner-subject vol (e.de-name)
    runner-walk vol es (i + 1) n (if err == 0 then bad else bad + 1)
   end

Section: Body

  opening : [Console, Device.Block] Nothing = act
    print-line-uni "=== DISK test runner ==="
    vol <- fat16-boot-volume
    print-line-uni ("volume    : bps=" & show (vol.vol-bytes-per-sector) & " ok=" & yn (vol.vol-bytes-per-sector == 512))
    if vol.vol-bytes-per-sector /= 512 then act
      print-line-uni "REFUSED   : no usable FAT16 volume; nothing to list"
      print-line-uni "=== runner done ==="
    end
    else act
    entries <- fat16-root-entries vol
    print-line-uni ("entries   : " & show (list-length entries) & " on the root")
    print-line-uni "note      : each entry is compiled ALONE, with no cite resolution, so a chapter carrying cites reports every cited name undefined. Only a cite-free chapter's verdict is comparable with test.ps1's."
    bad <- runner-walk vol entries 0 (list-length entries) 0
    print-line-uni ("summary   : failed=" & show bad)
    print-line-uni "=== runner done ==="
    end
  end

